<!--
  Directory.Build.targets — Veriqa-owned file (NOT a copy from .ai-shared/tooling/dotnet/,
  so `git subtree pull` does not overwrite it).

  Four purposes, all of which need a root file that a subtree sync cannot overwrite: guarding the
  chain version, suppressing Veriqa's own experimental-API diagnostics for this repository, keeping
  the dependency lists of the published packages as the projects state them, and pinning the
  dependencies between the core packages to one exact version.

  1. Version guard. Both the value (VeriqaCoreVersion) and its mapping
  onto <Version> live in build/Versions.props, but the <Import> that brings that file in lives
  in Directory.Build.props, which IS overwritten by a subtree sync. Because that Import is the
  only version-scheme line of the overwritable file, an empty VeriqaCoreVersion is an exact
  detector of the loss, and restoring the Import is a complete fix for the version scheme —
  but only for it: that file carries other local edits too (analyzer settings, the path to
  BannedSymbols.txt), which this guard does not watch. Without the guard such a sync would
  silently drop the version scheme and every package would be released under the SDK default
  version 1.0.0.

  2. Experimental-API diagnostics. Veriqa marks the extension points whose shape has not settled
  with [Experimental], and that marker is a compile error at every use site — which is the whole
  point of it for an integrator. This repository is not an integrator: it owns those types, and its
  own projects (core, tests, samples, sandbox) use them everywhere. So the suppression lives here,
  once, instead of a NoWarn in thirty project files or a #pragma around every use — and here rather
  than in Directory.Build.props, which `git subtree pull` overwrites.

  3. Transitive pinning of a published package. NuGet writes every pin into the nuspec as a direct
  dependency, which flattens the graph of the chain: a package would declare as its own every
  package listed in Directory.Packages.props that it reaches through a reference. IsPackable is
  stated in the project body, so this is the first file that can read it. A security pin a package has
  to pass on to its consumers is therefore a PackageReference without a version in that project.

  4. Exact versions between core packages. NuGet pack writes a ProjectReference as a minimum version
  ("0.7.1" means ">= 0.7.1"), so an integrator could combine a channel package with a newer core it was
  not built against. The channel packages and the store packages use internal members of the core
  (InternalsVisibleTo), and the documentation asks to keep every Veriqa.Core.* package on one version.
  So a reference to a Veriqa.Core.* project becomes an exact range "[0.7.1]" and restore refuses a
  mismatch. NuGet has no public switch for this: the target rewrites ProjectVersion on
  _ProjectReferencesWithVersions, the item that the internal NuGet pack target
  _GetProjectReferenceVersions fills and the PackTask reads. Other references keep their ranges.
-->
<Project>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!-- See purpose 2 in the header above. What the identifier marks and what it buys:
         build/README.md, "Public surface that has not settled yet". -->
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);VERIQAEXP0001</NoWarn>
    <!-- See purpose 3 in the header above. -->
    <CentralPackageTransitivePinningEnabled Condition=" '$(IsPackable)' == 'true' ">false</CentralPackageTransitivePinningEnabled>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <Target Name="VeriqaValidateCoreVersion" BeforeTargets="Build;Pack">
    <Error Condition=" '$(VeriqaCoreVersion)' == '' "
           Code="VERIQA0001"
           Text="VeriqaCoreVersion is not defined: build/Versions.props was not imported. Restore the &lt;Import Project=&quot;%24(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)build/Versions.props&quot; /&gt; line in the root Directory.Build.props — it is dropped by `git subtree pull` of .ai-shared. That line is the whole fix for the version scheme: build/Versions.props carries both VeriqaCoreVersion and the &lt;Version&gt; it feeds. It is not the only local edit of Directory.Build.props though, so merge the sync as a diff of that whole file. Without the Import every package would ship the SDK default version 1.0.0." />
  </Target>

  <!-- See purpose 4 in the header above. -->
  <Target Name="VeriqaPinCoreReferenceVersions" AfterTargets="_GetProjectReferenceVersions">
    <ItemGroup>
      <_ProjectReferencesWithVersions Condition=" $([System.String]::Copy('%(_ProjectReferencesWithVersions.Filename)').StartsWith('Veriqa.Core.')) and '%(_ProjectReferencesWithVersions.ProjectVersion)' != '' ">
        <ProjectVersion>[%(_ProjectReferencesWithVersions.ProjectVersion)]</ProjectVersion>
      </_ProjectReferencesWithVersions>
    </ItemGroup>
  </Target>

</Project>
